Manually checked edge cases:
- Empty input: solve(0, []) starts with an empty queue; head === n is 0 === 0, returning true.
- Self-cycle: solve(1, [[0,0]]) has indegree [1], so nothing is queued; 0 !== 1 returns false.
- Duplicate prerequisites: solve(2, [[1,0],[1,0]]) starts with indegrees [0,2] and queue [0]. Processing 0 decrements course 1 twice, from 2 to 1 to 0, enqueueing it exactly once. Processing 1 makes head 2, returning true.
- Adversarial disconnected cycle: solve(4, [[1,0],[3,2],[2,3]]) starts with indegrees [0,1,1,1] and queue [0]. Processing 0 releases 1; processing 1 releases nothing. Courses 2 and 3 remain blocked, so head is 2 rather than 4, returning false.

The invariant is that indegree counts incoming edges from unprocessed courses. Each course is queued exactly when that count reaches zero; processing all n courses is therefore equivalent to having no prerequisite cycle.

