Copy each row, then iteratively flood-fill orthogonal neighbors, recoloring when queued to prevent revisits. If the colors already match, return the copy immediately; the original image remains unchanged.

```javascript
function solve(image, row, col, newColor) {
  const result = image.map(line => line.slice());
  if (row < 0 || row >= result.length ||
      col < 0 || col >= result[row].length) {
    return result;
  }

  const startColor = result[row][col];
  if (startColor === newColor) return result;

  const stack = [[row, col]];
  result[row][col] = newColor;
  const directions = [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1]];

  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const [r, c] = stack.pop();
    for (const [dr, dc] of directions) {
      const nr = r + dr;
      const nc = c + dc;
      if (nr >= 0 && nr < result.length &&
          nc >= 0 && nc < result[nr].length &&
          result[nr][nc] === startColor) {
        result[nr][nc] = newColor;
        stack.push([nr, nc]);
      }
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

Call `solve(image, row, col, newColor)` directly, using zero-based coordinates. For an R × C image, time and space are both O(RC), including the returned copy.

